Task 1.1: Define the relation schema
For each of the five roles in your theme, write the relation schema: the relation name, its attributes, and the domain of each attribute. State which attribute or attributes form the primary key.


Relation Name
users
Start with the users
Attributes
user_id
display_name
deactivated
Primary Key
user_id
Domains
user_id > INT, positive, NOT NULL
display_name > VARCHAR(32), NOT NULL
deactivated> BOOLEAN





Relation Name
posts
Users post stuff on social media!
Attributes
post_id
user_id
post_title
activity_flag
metric
deleted
Primary Key
post_id
Domains
post_id > INT, non-negative, NOT NULL
user_id > Foreign Key on users
post_title > VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL
activity_flag > 0 or 1 (BOOLEAN)
metric > DOUBLE, non-negative
deleted > BOOLEAN





Relation Name
likes
Likes come from users on specific events, at specific times
Attributes
user_id
post_id
timestamp
dwell_ms
Primary Key
Composite primary key: post_id + user_id
A user can like a post once, and only once = unique
Domains
user_id > Foreign Key on users.user_id
post_id > Foreign Key on posts.post_id
timestamp > time
dwell_ms > integer, NOT NULL





Relation Name
hashtags
This stores the hashtag puts in each post; each post can have many hashtags
Attributes
post_id
hashtag
Primary Key
composite primary key between post_id + hashtag
Domains
post_id > Foreign Key on posts.post_id
hashtag > VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL





Relation Name
hashtag_catalogue
Maps individual hashtags like #GoGiants to “Sports”
Attributes
hashtag
category
Primary Key
composite key: hashtag + category
Domains
hashtag > VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL
category > VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL



